  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 457 AM EDT SAT OCT 31 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, a well defined shortwave \nwill be located just west of the forecast area. Otherwise, \nmidlevel ridging will dominate much of the eastern CONUS; \nmeanwhile, farther west, a substantial trough will be located over\nthe Intermountain West. At the surface, high pressure will sit \nover the Northeast, with a cold air damming pattern on the other \nside of the Appalachians. For our area, this pattern will favor \nsoutheasterly downslope winds, which should work to suppress \ndewpoints somewhat, even as total column moisture increases (with \nPW values surpassing 1 inch in most locations, anomalously moist \ncompared to climatology). Despite the size of this shortwave, \nheight falls won't be particularly intense, and this, in \ncombination with meager low level moisture (dewpoints in the upper\n30s and low 40s), should work against any widespread rain, \nparticularly east of I-75. The best chance for appreciable rain \nwill be in our southwestern counties, where low level moisture \nshould be more robust, with rain chances and QPF generally \ndecreasing closer to Virginia and West Virginia. Even in our \nsouthwestern counties, however, a lack of instability should keep \nrain totals unimpressive.\n\nFollowing the passage of this rather unimpressive shortwave, a \nmore substantial system will arrive next week, bringing more \nwidespread rain chances. Aloft, ridging will attempt to quickly \nbuild back in by late Monday, though a second and more subtle \nshortwave will cross through the area later in the day, with \nassociated cloud cover keeping lows fairly warm in the upper 40s \nand low 50s. Should this wave be weaker than currently modeled, it\nis possible some clearing allows valleys to cool slightly more \nthan is currently forecast; however, given ensemble preference \ntowards a mostly cloudy sky, terrain distinctions have been \nneglected for now. Tuesday will also be dry during the day as \nridging amplifies aloft over our area, with the aforementioned \ndeep trough to the west now featuring two distinct closed lows \naloft: one in the Upper Midwest and another farther south near the\nSouthern Plains, although there is significant model discrepancy \nregarding the positioning and evolution of this southern feature. \nAt the surface, an area of low pressure associated with this \npotent system will push northeast, with southeasterly to southerly\nwinds locally ahead of this system, but atmospheric moisture will\nbe very high, with LREF mean PW values in the range of 1.1-1.3 \ninches, firmly above the 90th percentile according to climatology.\nThis moist environment and strong source of forcing will set the \nstage for widespread rainfall, with the highest rain chances \ncurrently on Wednesday and continuing into Thursday. With this \nsystem, a tight pressure gradient will be in place, with fairly \nstrong flow just above the surface; despite sizable model \nuncertainty, LREF mean 925 mb flow is around 25-30 kt for much of \nthe forecast area, indicating increasing confidence in breezy \nconditions for our highest terrain as this system moves through. \nEven so, despite such potent kinematics, meager instability should\nlimit any threat of severe weather. \n\nLooking ahead, PoPs look to decrease on Friday, with a trend\ntowards cooler weather. However, immense model spread regarding\nthe evolution of this system adds considerable uncertainty by the\nend of the period.",
